The second best. Still, Rogues do not have such a tremendous advantage
here. Role difficulty is almost entirely based upon the early game -
characters who make it to the castle tend to be very similar afterwards.
For pre-castle Rogues, nothing is as certain as you make it out:

* You are highly unlikely to enchant the daggers to +7.

* You may only get 6 starting daggers, and while daggers are common in the
mines, they are not guaranteed, and those you find may not stack. You have
no initial rustproofing, leading to a possible reduction in your stack
size or its effectiveness.

* The spell of cause fear is irrelevant: Rogues are restricted in
enchantment. The only scaring method available in the early game is the
dust Elbereth: unreliable and ineffective against a significant class of
monsters (@).

* Rogues have the most variable of all starting stats; you are likely to
be rather weak.

* Rogues are chaotic, meaning: hostile mines, no easy early artifact, and
no access to the neutral artifacts.

* Thrown daggers pose the significant tactical problem of having to go and
pick them up again.

This last point is the most important one. Rogues gain no advantage while
killing popcorn monsters, as all roles are able to kill them easily, but
for tougher monsters, every turn counts. Moving from your advantageous
position to go and pick up your daggers wastes turns. A pet may pick them
up, wasting turns. A monster may pick them up, wasting turns. A monster
may throw them back at you, perhaps saving a few turns, but causing yet
more damage, and you still have to spend a turn to pick them up. Rogues
don't automatically get intrinsic speed either, so you get no relief from
the speed system. You may even fall through a trapdoor before being
able to pick them up, leaving you unarmed on an unexplored level.

Funnelling monsters through a bottleneck is an exceptionally important
tactic - in the castle, in Ludios, in throne rooms and treasure zoos, and
in general whenever you face a horde. It's no good if you have to throw
your daggers into your kill zone, then expose yourself to the horde to
pick them up.

Daggers are hard to use against sea monsters or when a peaceful monster
lies behind a hostile one. They may fall down the stairs or be teleported
away or eaten while on the floor, and you can't use them in shops without
having to repurchase them. 


Put simply, the advantages of the Rogue are all in theory. Maybe the RNG
will treat you nicely, but making /that/ assumption is why I called melee
combat only the second best way to die. The advantages of the Valkyrie are
solid, and of greater practical use:

* The best starting stats of all: high constitution means fast HP gain
means better damage absorption.

* Excellent starting armor which is easy to improve (unlike the Knight).

* Intrinsic speed at XL 7 (4/3 more damage dealt, 4/3 less damage taken!).

* The easiest quest for one of the best quest artifacts.

* Easy and early access to both Excalibur and Mjollnir.

And the kicker is that Valks can get to Expert in dagger too.

